{'_client': <twikit.client.client.Client object at 0x000001E534E2AE40>, '_data': {'__typename': 'Tweet', 'rest_id': '1877408220030869565', 'core': {'user_results': {'result': {'__typename': 'User', 'id': 'VXNlcjoxMTE1NzQ5NjMzOTg3MzA1NDcy', 'rest_id': '1115749633987305472', 'affiliates_highlighted_label': {}, 'has_graduated_access': True, 'is_blue_verified': True, 'profile_image_shape': 'Circle', 'legacy': {'can_dm': True, 'can_media_tag': True, 'created_at': 'Tue Apr 09 22:53:37 +0000 2019', 'default_profile': True, 'default_profile_image': False, 'description': 'Retired Royal Canadian Marine Search & Rescue Specialist. Happily married to a compassionate and dedicated frontline Doctor with 3  amazing, talented children.', 'entities': {'description': {'urls': []}}, 'fast_followers_count': 0, 'favourites_count': 197141, 'followers_count': 11915, 'friends_count': 9798, 'has_custom_timelines': True, 'is_translator': False, 'listed_count': 8, 'location': '', 'media_count': 5181, 'name': 'Barry Sharp', 'normal_followers_count': 11915, 'pinned_tweet_ids_str': ['1878838948962951297'], 'possibly_sensitive': False, 'profile_banner_url': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_banners/1115749633987305472/1722786870', 'profile_image_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1820126123474538496/wurAA1jZ_normal.jpg', 'profile_interstitial_type': '', 'screen_name': 'BarryESharp', 'statuses_count': 199826, 'translator_type': 'none', 'verified': False, 'want_retweets': False, 'withheld_in_countries': []}}}}, 'unmention_data': {}, 'edit_control': {'edit_tweet_ids': ['1877408220030869565'], 'editable_until_msecs': '1736447574000', 'is_edit_eligible': False, 'edits_remaining': '5'}, 'is_translatable': False, 'views': {'count': '46', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="http://twitter.com/download/iphone" rel="nofollow">Twitter for iPhone</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4Nzc0MDgyMTk3Mjg4Mzg2NTY=', 'text': "While the Jacobson v. Massachusetts case was primarily a U.S. legal precedent, its principles have influenced discussions on public health mandates internationally.\n\nLegal Case Against Globally Applying Jacobson v. Massachusetts to mRNA/DNA Vaccine Mandates:\n\n[Case Title: Sharp et al. v. Attorney General of Canada]\n\nThe decision in Jacobson v. Massachusetts (1905) cannot be directly applied to modern mandates for mRNA and DNA vaccines due to several key differences:\n\n1. Nature of the Vaccine:\n-Smallpox Vaccine vs. mRNA/DNA Vaccines: The smallpox vaccine was a well-established method by 1905, having been in use for over a century with known efficacy and safety profiles. In contrast, mRNA and DNA vaccines were experimental at the time of mandate implementation, with limited long-term safety and efficacy data. This introduces significant differences in risk assessment and public health justification\n\n2. Public Health Context: \n- Disease and Transmission: Smallpox was a highly contagious and deadly disease with no known cure, making vaccination a direct, effective means of controlling outbreaks. mRNA/DNA vaccines were introduced during the COVID-19 crisis, where the virus had variable transmission rates, efficacy against variants was uncertain, and vaccines did not always prevent transmission. This questions the proportionality and necessity of mandates.\n\n3. Consent and Coercion:\n- Informed Consent: The Jacobson case was decided without modern concepts of informed consent as understood today, particularly under frameworks like the Nuremberg Code. The imposition of mRNA/DNA vaccines under threat of job loss or social exclusion could be seen as coercive, potentially violating principles of voluntary consent.\n\n4. Legal and Ethical Frameworks:\n- Modern Legal Rights: Today's legal landscape includes stronger protections for individual rights, including privacy, bodily autonomy, and the right to refuse medical treatment. The Charter of Rights and Freedoms in Canada, for instance, provides protections that were not part of any legal discussion in 1905. Applying Jacobson overlooks these evolved legal standards.\n\n5. Scientific Understanding and Evidence:\n-Efficacy and Safety: The smallpox vaccine had a long track record of preventing the disease. mRNA/DNA vaccines, however, faced scrutiny over their effectiveness against new variants and potential side effects. The reliance on observational rather than randomized control trials for assessing real-world efficacy adds layers of complexity not present in the Jacobson era\n\n6. Public Health Justification:\n- Proportionality and Balancing: Jacobson emphasized the balance between public health and individual rights. However, with mRNA/DNA vaccines, the balance might not hold as the vaccines did not prevent transmission or acquisition as effectively as hoped or if there are significant known risks. The public health benefit vs. infringement on rights might not be proportional.\n\nGiven these points, the legal and ethical standards have evolved significantly since Jacobson, making the direct application of that precedent to modern vaccine mandates inappropriate. The case for mRNA/DNA vaccine mandates must be assessed under contemporary legal, ethical, and scientific criteria, which differ substantially from the context of early 20th-century public health measures.\n\nThus, applying Jacobson directly to modern vaccine mandates ignores significant legal, ethical, and scientific evolution.", 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Thu Jan 09 17:32:54 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1877347490887811322', 'display_text_range': [14, 292], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'indices': [0, 13]}]}, 'favorite_count': 0, 'favorited': False, 'f